Pod remains Pending|A new Pod never receives a node assignment.|kubectl describe pod POD -n NAMESPACE|Read scheduling conditions and events for requests, taints, affinity, topology or unbound claims. Aggregate idle CPU does not prove a feasible node exists.|Correct the evidenced request or placement mistake, provision appropriate capacity or resolve storage binding. Preserve intentional isolation constraints.|Confirm scheduling, readiness and application health, and verify the Pod landed in the intended failure domain.
CrashLoopBackOff|A container repeatedly starts and exits.|kubectl logs POD -n NAMESPACE --previous --tail=100|Combine previous logs with last termination state and events. Distinguish application exit, OOM, failed liveness and missing configuration.|Fix or roll back the evidenced application/configuration regression; tune resources or probes only when supported by measurements.|Verify restart count stabilizes and representative traffic succeeds across the rollout, including startup and shutdown.
ImagePullBackOff|A Pod cannot retrieve its configured image.|kubectl describe pod POD -n NAMESPACE|Read pull error details and confirm image reference, digest/tag, registry authorization and node network path. Different errors require different fixes.|Correct the reference or image-pull identity, restore registry reachability or publish the intended image. Avoid embedding registry credentials in manifests.|Confirm the intended image digest runs and all eligible nodes can pull through the approved path.
Service has no ready backends|A Service exists but requests have no available backend.|kubectl get endpointslices -n NAMESPACE -l kubernetes.io/service-name=SERVICE -o yaml|Compare Service selectors with Pod labels and endpoint readiness. Inspect readiness-probe failures before assuming a networking defect.|Correct selector mismatch or restore application readiness. Do not bypass readiness merely to make an endpoint appear.|Verify ready EndpointSlices and a successful request through the Service from an intended client.
Service port mismatch|Pods are ready but connections through the Service fail.|kubectl describe service SERVICE -n NAMESPACE|Compare port and targetPort with the actual container listener, including named-port resolution. Confirm endpoints before investigating policy.|Correct the Service mapping or application bind port and roll out the change in source.|Test the Service port and direct backend path from an authorized diagnostic client and confirm only intended exposure.
DNS blocked by egress policy|A workload loses name resolution after a restrictive policy rollout.|kubectl get networkpolicy -n NAMESPACE -o yaml|Inspect policies selecting the Pod and the actual DNS path, including the cluster's DNS implementation. Confirm whether UDP/TCP DNS traffic is allowed as required.|Add narrowly scoped DNS egress compatible with the cluster design while retaining dependency restrictions.|Resolve required names from the workload and confirm unrelated outbound traffic remains blocked.
PVC remains Pending|A storage-backed workload cannot start because its claim is unbound.|kubectl describe pvc CLAIM -n NAMESPACE|Inspect StorageClass, access mode, capacity, provisioner events and topology. WaitForFirstConsumer may intentionally defer binding until scheduling context exists.|Correct an invalid class/request or restore provisioner permissions/capacity. Do not create an arbitrary volume in the wrong zone.|Confirm binding, attachment, mount and an application-level read/write check on the intended durable storage.
HPA does not scale|An HPA stays at its current replica count during increased demand.|kubectl describe hpa APP -n NAMESPACE|Read conditions and metric status; check resource requests for utilization-based targets, bounds and stabilization. Verify the workload is horizontally scalable.|Restore missing metrics or requests, correct the target/bounds and address downstream limits. Raising max replicas alone may not help.|Apply representative load and verify replica changes, readiness and user latency while preserving dependency headroom.
Drain blocked by disruption budget|Node maintenance cannot evict a Pod.|kubectl get pdb -n NAMESPACE|Compare budget selector, allowed disruptions, ready replicas and application quorum. An unavailable replica may leave no safe eviction capacity.|Restore readiness or add justified replicas before drain. Change the budget only when application availability semantics permit it.|Confirm a controlled eviction succeeds while required quorum and user service remain healthy.
Rollout stalls on readiness|A Deployment creates new Pods but never becomes fully available.|kubectl rollout status deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE --timeout=60s|Inspect Deployment conditions, new ReplicaSet Pods and readiness logs. Check configuration, dependencies and surge capacity rather than increasing deadlines blindly.|Fix the failing revision or perform a compatible rollback. Correct probe design if it does not reflect actual readiness.|Verify rollout completion and representative user requests, including all replicas and any affected dependency paths.
